arXiv:2509.09728v1 [cs.CL] 10 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Elena Rohde
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Institute of Sociology, Faculty of Social Sciences University of Duisburg-Essen,Lotharstr. 65, 47057 Duisburg, Germany
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jonas Klingwort
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Research &amp; Development, Statistics Netherlands (CBS), CBS-weg 11,PO Box 4481, 6401 CZ Heerlen, the Netherlands
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christian Borgs
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>IT.NRW – Statistical Office of NRW, Mauerstr. 51, 40476 Düsseldorf, Germany<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>correspondence: elena.rohde@stud.uni-due.de
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents a meta-analysis evaluating ML performance in sentiment analysis for Twitter data. The study aims to estimate the average performance, assess heterogeneity between and within studies, and analyze how study characteristics influence model performance. Using PRISMA guidelines, we searched academic databases and selected 195 trials from 20 studies with 12 study features. Overall accuracy, the most reported performance metric, was analyzed using double arcsine transformation and a three-level random effects model. The average overall accuracy of the AIC-optimized model was 0.80 <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.76,0.84]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.76</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.84</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.76,0.84]</annotation></semantics></math>. This paper provides two key insights: 1) Overall accuracy is widely used but often misleading due to its sensitivity to class imbalance and the number of sentiment classes, highlighting the need for normalization. 2) Standardized reporting of model performance, including reporting confusion matrices for independent test sets, is essential for reliable comparisons of ML classifiers across studies, which seems far from common practice.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Social media is a valuable data source for social science research, particularly in analyzing public sentiment during events with considerable social impact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021c</a>)</cite>. However, the large volume of text data makes evaluation challenging. Sentiment analysis, using Natural Language Processing, extracts attitudes and emotions from text to classify content into categories like positive, negative, or neutral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Govindarajan, 2022c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Sentiment analysis methods fall into lexicon-based and machine-learning approaches, with the latter preferred for social media due to higher accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Verma &amp; Jain, 2022c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2019c</a>)</cite>. Machine learning strategies vary by algorithm and feature extraction, making overall performance evaluation challenging. This raises questions about algorithm effectiveness and the factors influencing variability. Identifying study characteristics and potential variability sources is crucial for setting realistic performance expectations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper contributes to the literature by conducting a systematic literature review, followed by a meta-analysis and meta-regression, to explain the variation in the performance outcomes of machine learning algorithms in the context of social media data sentiment analysis. The results provide evidence of the factors contributing to the varying performance of different machine-learning algorithms in sentiment analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Research background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sentiment analysis is a method to analyze opinions, attitudes, and emotions towards entities expressed in text. The method was established in the early 1990s, extracting sentiment adjectives and analyzing viewpoints and metaphors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu, 2015c</a>)</cite>. In the early 2000s, sentiment analysis became more established and has been the subject of increasing research in recent years.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given the growing interest in sentiment analysis, understanding performance factors is important. Key factors include training data size, balance, language, and text length. Larger datasets improve accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi &amp; Lee, 2017c</a>)</cite>, while unbalanced data yields higher accuracy than balanced datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>. English texts are classified more accurately than Dutch or French <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boiy &amp; Moens, 2009c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>. Longer texts aid classification, especially with advanced models like BERT. Model and algorithm choices also impact performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuller et al., 2015c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Advanced techniques, including neural networks and ensemble methods like the extra tree classifier, tend to outperform traditional methods like support vector machines or decision trees in sentiment classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rustam et al., 2021c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stine, 2019c</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, the achieved performance is also affected by the effectiveness of the feature extraction methods. Finally, fewer sentiment classes generally result in better model performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bouazizi &amp; Ohtsuki, 2019c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>. To sum up, recent studies identify several key factors that affect classification quality, including the number of sentiment classes, text length and language, machine learning method, feature extraction technique, training dataset size, and sentiment class distribution within the training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Few studies systematically analyze sentiment analysis performance factors. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bibe.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al. (2019c)</a></cite> examines method performance across social media datasets, while the only meta-analysis by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al. (2023c)</a></cite> reports an average accuracy of 78%, highlighting study-specific variability. Despite the growing number of publications, the lack of meta-analyses underscores the need for such studies to identify trends, generalize findings, and assess heterogeneity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boiy &amp; Moens, 2009c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al., 2023c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This paper aims to analyze how study designs affect the performance of machine learning algorithms in sentiment analysis and estimate the expected machine learning performance. In contrast to the existing meta-analysis from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al. (2023c)</a></cite>, the focus is on data from Twitter only. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bibe.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al. (2023c)</a></cite> compare lexicon approaches, traditional machine learning algorithms, and transfer learning models, this paper compares a more differentiated feature about machine learning algorithms. Furthermore, this paper uses additional features potentially affecting classification quality or potentially indicating the overall quality of the published paper, such as the labeling method, size of the majority class, or whether a confusion matrix was reported.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methods</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A systematic literature review was conducted to identify relevant papers providing evidence for the research question (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Systematic literature review and article selection ‣ 3 Methods ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). The review is followed by a meta-analysis and meta-regression (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Statistical analysis ‣ 3 Methods ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Systematic literature review and article selection</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Inclusion criteria</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, to adhere to our inclusion criteria, studies that perform sentiment analysis with machine learning techniques on Twitter data must report a quality metric: classification accuracy. Accuracy was chosen because it was reported in most of the identified studies. Second, the studies must have been peer-reviewed and published in journals, conference proceedings, or collective monographs. Third, the search was restricted to the last year, in which the social media platform Twitter had its original name (2022). As of July 2023, the platform’s name changed to ‘X’ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah &amp; Martin, 2023c</a>)</cite>. Although the change of platform policies might be an interesting feature, this decision was made to narrow down the scope of this meta-analysis. Fourth, only studies written in English are included. Fifth, by the definition of sentiment analysis in chapter <a href="#S2" title="2 Research background ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, only concepts representing an opinion, attitude, judgment, or emotion were considered sentiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Identification</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three large and relevant databases in the field of survey methodology and the social sciences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schnell &amp; Thomas, 2021c</a>)</cite> were considered: Scopus, Web of Science, and Google Scholar. The search term <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“<span id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_innerquote">‘machine learning classifier’</span> AND <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_innerquote">‘sentiment analysis’</span> AND <span id="S3.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_innerquote">‘twitter’</span>”</span> was used. The process of identifying records according to the PRISMA guidelines is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Identification ‣ 3.1 Systematic literature review and article selection ‣ 3 Methods ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Finally, 411 records were identified. Gray literature, duplicates, non-english texts, books, and unreferenced documents were removed, leaving 365 records for abstract screening.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09728v1/figures_pdf/prisma.PNG" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:286/271;" width="286" height="271" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Process of identifying records in a systematic literature search according to the PRISMA 2021 statement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Page et al., 2021c</a>)</cite>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Screening: abstracts</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the abstracts indicated a study using machine learning algorithms for sentiment analysis of Twitter data, they qualified for further review. For screening, the ‘metagear’ R-library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lajeunesse, 2016c</a>)</cite> was used with R version 4.3.3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">R Core Team, 2024c</a>)</cite>. General and specific keywords were chosen, and keywords that indicate inclusion and exclusion were selected. All authors conducted the screening independently to strengthen the quality and objectivity of this screening process. After screening, 278 studies were considered as not eligible.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Screening: predatory journals</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remaining 87 studies were checked for signs of predatory behavior. Beall’s list and checklist <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ross-White et al., 2019c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beall, 2015c</a>)</cite> were used for this screening procedure. In total, 16 records were excluded, leaving 71 for a full-text eligibility assessment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Screening: full texts</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following study attributes should be included in the dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cuijpers, 2016c</a>)</cite>: study characteristics and features and information needed to calculate effect sizes. 51 records were excluded for the reasons shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Identification ‣ 3.1 Systematic literature review and article selection ‣ 3 Methods ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, leaving 20 records. These studies published the classification quality of multiple trials, resulting in <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=</annotation></semantics></math> 195 reported overall accuracies distributed over the <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=20</annotation></semantics></math> studies. Each study contributes a minimum of 2 and a maximum of 39 observations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSSx6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Screening: study features</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Twelve features were derived from the selected studies. Two could be used as numeric features (see Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Screening: study features ‣ 3.1 Systematic literature review and article selection ‣ 3 Methods ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The feature ‘Size of the training data set (scaled)’ was scaled by the factor <math id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e3</annotation></semantics></math>. Ten features were used as categorical (see Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Screening: study features ‣ 3.1 Systematic literature review and article selection ‣ 3 Methods ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The feature ‘Number of sentiment classes’ was categorized to avoid sparse categories. The ‘Number of feature extraction methods’ and ‘Majority class’ had to be categorized and could not be used as numeric features because some studies did not provide this information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSSx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The feature ‘Training and test set ratio’ is the size of training set divided by size of the test set. The category ‘Other’ was introduced to group rare occasions to avoid sparse categories. For ‘Tweet language’, the languages Nepali, Italian, and Tamil were grouped in ‘Other’. For ‘Tweet topic’, topics on LGBTQ and railway infrastructure were grouped in ‘Other’. For the ‘Tweet labeling method,’ a pre-labeled dataset or a combination of manual and machine-learning-based labeling was grouped in ‘Other.’ For ‘Feature extraction methods’, the method combining Bag of Words and TF-IDF, Count Vector, N-Grams, GloVe, and Bert Tokenizer was grouped in ‘Other’. If the information could not be retrieved, this was coded as ‘Not specified’. For the feature ‘Machine-learning model,’ Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes, Multinomial Naive Bayes, and Genetic Algorithm were grouped as ‘Classical Machine-learning.’</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09728v1/01_numeric_features.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:395/132;" width="395" height="132"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of overall accuracy by numeric feature.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09728v1/01_categorical_features.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:395/362;" width="395" height="362"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of overall accuracy by categorical feature.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Statistical analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A meta-analysis of proportions is a statistical method used to synthesize and analyze results from multiple studies reporting proportions or rates, such as prevalence or incidence rates. Transferred to the current study, we aim to synthesize overall accuracies from various machine learning studies. For the statistical analyses, the R packages <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">metafor</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Viechtbauer, 2010c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Viechtbauer, 2024c</a>)</cite> and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dmetar</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrer et al., 2019c</a>)</cite> were used. The content and formulas in this section are based on the paper by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bibe.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leach et al., under reviewc (under review)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Transforming proportions</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper uses the Freeman-Tukey double arcsine transformation (FT) due to reported accuracies near 1.0, which cause variance instability under the logit transformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Freeman &amp; Tukey, 1950c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warton &amp; Hui, 2011c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwarzer et al., 2019c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barendregt et al., 2013c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang, 2023c</a>)</cite>. Other transformations, including arcsine square root, logit, and log, showed greater deviations than the double arcsine from normality (tested via Shapiro-Wilk). The double arcsine transformation was applied to the observed overall accuracy (<math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>) in trial <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of study <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in the observed individual effect size, <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\theta}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\theta}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its sampling variance <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{k_{ij}}{n_{ij}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{k_{ij}}{n_{ij}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\theta}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\theta}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{FT}(p_{ij})=\frac{\sin^{-1}\sqrt{\frac{k_{ij}}{n_{ij}+1}}+\sin^{-1}\sqrt{\frac{k_{ij}+1}{n_{ij}+1}}}{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>FT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msqrt><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{FT}(p_{ij})=\frac{\sin^{-1}\sqrt{\frac{k_{ij}}{n_{ij}+1}}+\sin^{-1}\sqrt{\frac{k_{ij}+1}{n_{ij}+1}}}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1/(4n_{ij}+2)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1/(4n_{ij}+2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> being the number of correctly classified instances and <math id="S3.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of instances in trial <math id="S3.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of study <math id="S3.SSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The transformed values are used to compute the summary effect size and standard error, with results back-transformed into raw proportions for easier reporting. For the back-transformation using the inverse population variance, we refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bibe.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barendregt et al. (2013c)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Meta-analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The independence among effect sizes is a key assumption in most meta-analytic models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheung, 2014c</a>)</cite>. Hence, a multilevel random effect model was used to estimate the population effect size and heterogeneity between and within the studies, accounting for the dependency of individual effect sizes within each study <math id="S3.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. A random-effects model has to be chosen when the studies are expected to have different underlying effects, often due to variability in study populations, interventions, or settings; it accounts for both within-study and between-study variation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, there is the individual level (1). Here, <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\theta}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\theta}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is the observed individual effect size from trial <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in study <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. This level is modeled as the (unobserved) true individual effect size <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> plus an error term <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. The error term is assumed to follow a normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a sampling variance of <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\theta}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\theta}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\theta_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\theta_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\epsilon_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\epsilon_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim N(0,v_{ij})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim N(0,v_{ij}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Second, there is the study level (2). Here, the true individual effect size <math id="S3.SSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> for trial <math id="S3.SSx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in study <math id="S3.SSx2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed as the study effect size <math id="S3.SSx2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> from study <math id="S3.SSx2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and the within-study heterogeneity <math id="S3.SSx2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. This heterogeneity is assumed to follow a normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a within-study variance of <math id="S3.SSx2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\kappa_{j}+\zeta_{ij}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\kappa_{j}+\zeta_{ij},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\zeta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\zeta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}_{\zeta})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Third, there is the population level (3). Here, the study effect size <math id="S3.SSx2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in study <math id="S3.SSx2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is modeled as the population effect size, <math id="S3.SSx2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, and between-study heterogeneity <math id="S3.SSx2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This heterogeneity is assumed to follow a normal distribution with mean 0 and between-study variance <math id="S3.SSx2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\kappa_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\kappa_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mu+\xi_{j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mu+\xi_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\xi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\xi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}_{\xi})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}_{\xi}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The observed individual effect size is decomposed into the population effect size, the between-study heterogeneity, the within-study heterogeneity, and an error component: <math id="S3.SSx2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\theta}_{ij}=\mu+\xi_{j}+\zeta_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\theta}_{ij}=\mu+\xi_{j}+\zeta_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its total variance is <math id="S3.SSx2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(\tilde{\theta}_{ij})=\sigma^{2}_{\xi}+\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}+v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(\tilde{\theta}_{ij})=\sigma^{2}_{\xi}+\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}+v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The population effect size <math id="S3.SSx2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted sum of the observed individual effect sizes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Konstantopoulos, 2011c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheung, 2014c</a>)</cite>. The weight considers the covariance between individual effect sizes within a study. The particular effect sizes in different studies are assumed to be independent, and individual effect sizes within a study share the same covariance. The restricted maximum likelihood (REML) is used to estimate the parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The total variance is expressed as <math id="S3.SSx2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(\tilde{\theta})=\sigma^{2}_{\xi}+\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}+\sigma^{2}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(\tilde{\theta})=\sigma^{2}_{\xi}+\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}+\sigma^{2}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SSx2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a pooled estimate of the sampling variances <math id="S3.SSx2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Higgins &amp; Thompson, 2002c</a>)</cite> to evaluate the relative contributions of the three variance components to the total variance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Quantifying heterogeneity</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess potential heterogeneity, several metrics are used. First, <math id="S3.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math> quantify the variance of true effect sizes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrer et al., 2022c</a>)</cite>, informing about variation within studies (level 2) and between studies (level 3) in a three-level meta-analysis. Second, Cochran’s <math id="S3.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cochran, 1954c</a>)</cite> tests whether variation exceeds what is expected from sampling error. Third, the <math id="S3.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> statistic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Higgins &amp; Thompson, 2002c</a>)</cite> measures the proportion of variability not due to sampling error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrer et al., 2022c</a>)</cite>. The metrics <math id="S3.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate how much of the observed variation exceeds the expected variation under homogeneity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Meta regression</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Meta-regression is a type of moderator analysis that attempts to explain the identified heterogeneity by examining the patterns of heterogeneity in the data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang, 2023c</a>; <a href="#bibe.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrer et al., 2022c</a>)</cite>. Meta-regression can be described as the study-level equivalent of regression analysis in individual-level data, as study features are independent variables and the observed effect size is the dependent variable to be predicted <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bibe.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrer et al., 2022c</a>)</cite>. The study features might explain the potentially identified heterogeneity between and within studies. For this purpose, the multilevel model is extended and becomes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\theta}_{ij}=\mu+\bm{x}_{j}^{\intercal}\bm{\beta}_{j}+\xi_{j}+\zeta_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊺</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\theta}_{ij}=\mu+\bm{x}_{j}^{\intercal}\bm{\beta}_{j}+\xi_{j}+\zeta_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SSx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\beta}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\beta}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> being a vector of <math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> regression coefficients of study study <math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{j}^{\intercal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊺</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{j}^{\intercal}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transpose of a vector. The heterogeneity <math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{j}+\zeta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{j}+\zeta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is now the variability across the true individual effect sizes <math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> that is not explained by the features. Adding features to the model will reduce the variances at the population level (<math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>) and study level (<math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>). The reduction is quantified by <math id="S3.SSx4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R^{2}_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R^{2}_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-\frac{\sigma^{2}_{\xi}(X)}{\sigma^{2}_{\xi}(0)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-\frac{\sigma^{2}_{\xi}(X)}{\sigma^{2}_{\xi}(0)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R^{2}_{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R^{2}_{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-\frac{\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}(X)}{\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}(0)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-\frac{\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}(X)}{\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}(0)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SSx4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SSx4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the variance according to the model with features and <math id="S3.SSx4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> the variance according to the null model. Five models will be fitted; the first is the null model. Second, the full model uses all the collected features. Third, a model optimized towards the AIC; fourth, a model optimized towards the BIC; and fifth, a model optimized towards the RMSE will be shown. The null model serves as a baseline and will inform on the potential improvements of the other models. The complete model uses the twelve collected features. The AIC-optimized model balances fit and complexity. The BIC-optimized model has a stronger penalty for model complexity than AIC, reducing the risk of overfitting. The RMSE-optimized model does not penalize complexity, meaning a more complex model with lower RMSE might still be overfitting.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, the results of the meta-analysis and meta-regression are shown. This includes the review of the summary effect size, the quantification of heterogeneity, and the subsequent meta-regression.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Three-level meta-analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The forest plot in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Three-level meta-analysis ‣ 4 Results ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the 20 individual studies, how many trials each study contributes, and their average summary effect size. The weighted average of the overall accuracy is estimated at 0.8 (95% CI: 0.75;0.85). Cochran’s Q-test yielded a significant result (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=518042" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>518042</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=518042</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating the presence of heterogeneity beyond chance, i.e., sampling error. The variance of the true effect sizes indicates heterogeneity between and within studies (see Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Three-level meta-regression ‣ 4 Results ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The within-study variation attributable to the second level is higher, accounting for about 29% of the data variation. Most of the variation in the data is due to between-study heterogeneity at level three, with about 71%. All measures suggest that a large proportion of the heterogeneity in the data is not due to sampling error and that there is substantial heterogeneity between studies. These results justify the application of a meta-regression to attempt to explain the identified heterogeneity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09728v1/forest_nullmodel.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/339;" width="476" height="339"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Forest plot of three-level meta-analysis.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Three-level meta-regression</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Three-level meta-regression ‣ 4 Results ‣ A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows five fitted models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:46.8pt;vertical-align:-21.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-97.1pt,13.2pt) scale(0.639941291617806,0.639941291617806) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Model</th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">AIC</th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">BIC</th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">RMSE</th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=20</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>195</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=195</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> [<math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{l};\hat{\mu}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{l};\hat{\mu}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>]</th>
